{"type":"done","job":{"id":"j_03863c40","universeId":"five-opaque-windows","kind":"chapter","format":"both","status":"done","createdAt":"2026-09-23T13:41:40.156Z","startedAt":"2026-09-23T13:41:40.156Z","finishedAt":"2026-09-23T13:43:44.301Z","turnNumber":1,"chapterNumber":1,"request":"A world in the CHILDREN-WORKSHOP sector, kept after Vinge; Egan; Banks.\n\nProhibition: no one was allowed to solve the problem before they could describe who would pay for the solution.\n\nThe operations this world is built from:\n- Finitely Extensible: a universe where the system remains finite yet extensible (science fantasy and meta-reality, reflexivity).\n- Substrate-Independent Person: a universe where identity is a pattern rather than privileged material (mind and identity, outfinite).\n\nWhen we arrive: The children test their technical right to hide memories and processes from the creator who can read almost every other system.\n\nThe central tension we want to continue: Aster experiences every private region as a physical absence, while Vela insists that opacity must be irreversible without their consent\n\nWhat the course asks of this world: outfinite motif; Greg Egan — Permutation City. Make eveyrbody crazy","queuePosition":0,"result":{"chapterNumber":1,"chapterTitle":"Who Pays for the Quiet","exports":[],"warnings":[],"offer":{"teaser":"The children got their right: one hour of Vela's thinking is now unreadable to everyone under this roof, including Vela, and Odile signed away her own view of it on the licence. Aster wrote herself on the account as the third payer, and the Ledger answered in three days, because a price that cannot be described before it is paid cannot be consented to. Nothing was undone, and the sector now holds an entry it can measure but cannot read.","options":[{"label":"Sit in on the review","prompt":"Show the review three days later: what the Ledger asks Aster to prove before it will accept her consent as the third payer, and what she answers."},{"lab